# TaskShapedMeshSeams.allow — the hub-reachable-async ratchet, rule 2 (#3433).
#
# Each line is a file under src/MeshWeaver.Mesh.Contract whose INTERFACE declares one or more
# Task/ValueTask-returning members.
#
# WHY THE SHAPE IS THE ROOT, not a style. The mesh contract is the SDK-free assembly that hub-side
# modules and agent tools compile against. A Task<T> on one of its interfaces does not merely
# permit an await at the call site — it FORCES one, because a Task has exactly one consumption
# idiom. IEaGraphAuth declared three, and ExecutiveAssistantPlugin (MeshWeaver.Plugins), an agent
# TOOL, therefore awaited a mesh read from inside an agent round: #3433. IObservable<T> is the one
# shape both a hub turn and an ASP.NET action can consume, because only the second of those can
# bridge, and it bridges once at its own edge.
#
# SCOPE is the DECLARATION. A class in that assembly may still hold Task-returning internals —
# IIoPool takes and returns them BY DESIGN, it is the one sanctioned boundary between the
# turn-based schedulers and real I/O. This rule does not look at classes at all.
#
# THIS RATCHET MAY ONLY SHRINK. Lower HubReachableAsyncGuard.SeamTotalBudget whenever you delete
# or lower an entry.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IEaGraphAuth is GONE from this list (#3433 closed). The three ...Async forwarders were deleted
# once MeshWeaver.Plugins stopped calling them and the shipping portal never had; the seam is
# reactive end to end. Do not re-add a Task-shaped member to it.
#
# ICrossSchemaQueryProvider — pre-existing, NOT investigated as part of #3433. It is listed
# because the rule is about the SHAPE, and seeding an inventory is how a ratchet distinguishes
# "already there" from "just added". Whether any of its five members is reached from a hub turn is
# an open question, not a claim made here; answering it is what would let this line go to 0.
src/MeshWeaver.Mesh.Contract/Services/ICrossSchemaQueryProvider.cs	5
